{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Forty stripes shall he give him, he shall not add.</b> This lashing is a judgment in its own right and is a law from Heaven: the judge is obligated to impose it and the sinner to endure it, and he may not add beyond the prescribed judgment, lest his fellow be degraded. And the Sages, may their memory be blessed, expounded in the chapter ואלו הן הלוקין: How do they flog him? They bind his two hands to the pillar, this way and that, and hold his garments until they bare his chest, and a stone is placed behind him, and the sexton of the synagogue stands upon it, with a strap of calf-hide in his hand — and this is why Scripture juxtaposes to this section ״לא תחסום שור״ — 'You shall not muzzle the ox.' And this strap is doubled into two, and two into four, and two straps of donkey-hide go up and down upon it; its handle is a handbreadth and its width is a handbreadth; and he flogs him one third from in front of him and two strokes from behind him — thus far. And the reason it was a handbreadth wide and was doubled and redoubled: because a handbreadth contains four fingerbreadths, and when doubled that is two handbreadths and eight fingerbreadths, and when folded over that is sixteen fingerbreadths. Therefore the prescribed measure of the lashing strap was to contain sixteen fingerbreadths, because there is no commandment written in the Torah upon which fewer than sixteen covenants were not cut; and this sinner who is punished with lashes transgressed a commandment upon which sixteen covenants were cut, and therefore he is lashed with a strap that contains sixteen fingerbreadths. And the reason two straps go up and down upon it: corresponding to נעשה ונשמע — 'we will do and we will hear.' And the reason they are of donkey-hide: in order to join together ox and donkey, as it is written (Isaiah 1:3): ״ידע שור קונהו וחמור אבוס בעליו ישראל לא ידע״ — 'The ox knows his owner, and the donkey his master's crib; Israel does not know' — for whoever transgresses a commandment and has become estranged from the kindnesses of HaShem and did not acknowledge them, it is fitting that he be punished and lashed with a strap of ox and donkey, which recognize their owners." "<b>And we find</b> in the Yerushalmi: From where do we know that the strap is doubled? As it is said, והפילו השופט — 'and the judge shall make him fall'; do not read והפילו but rather והכפילו — 'and he shall double it.' And the reason he flogs him one third from in front of him: from the verse that says והכהו לפניו כדי רשעתו — 'and he shall strike him before him according to the measure of his wickedness'; and the Sages, may their memory be blessed, expounded: כדי רשעה אחת — 'according to one measure of wickedness' — from in front, and two strokes from behind, corresponding to יכנו להכותו — 'they shall strike him, to strike him' — which are two strikings. Now it should have said, והכהו לפניו ארבעים לא יוסיף פן יוסיף על אלה — 'and he shall strike him before him; forty he shall not add, lest he add beyond these.' But Scripture mentioned three strikings — והכהו יכנו להכותו — to teach you that there is one striking from in front, as it says והכהו לפניו, and two from behind, as they are written in the latter part of the verse." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ארבעים יכנו — 'Forty shall he strike him' — why forty? This man was formed in forty days, and he transgressed the Torah that was given in forty days; let him be lashed forty and discharge his punishment. And likewise you find with the first man: when he was commanded and transgressed, the world was punished with forty — the punishment of man ten, of Eve ten, of the serpent ten, of the earth ten; therefore, when a man transgresses a commandment, let him be lashed forty. And likewise you find in the punishment of the spies, that Israel were punished forty years in the wilderness; thus the Sages, may their memory be blessed, expounded in Midrash Tanchuma in the section במדבר סיני (4) on the verse אל תכריתו." "<b>Lest he add.</b> From the fact that Scripture repeated and said פן יוסיף, the holy Rav, Rabbi Yonah, of blessed memory, proved from here that one who strikes his fellow or his wife transgresses two negative commandments: one is לא יוסיף, and the second is פן יוסיף, which is also a negative prohibition, since every place where השמר פן or אל is said is nothing but a negative commandment." "<b>A severe stripe.</b> If he adds even one, Scripture calls it מכה רבה — 'a severe stripe.' And there is a hint here in the word רבה, for the negative commandments are שס״ה — 365 — and among them are many prohibitions for which there is no obligation of lashes, as they said: a negative commandment that involves no action — one is not lashed for it; and those for which there is an obligation of lashes are ר״ז — 207 — prohibitions, which is the numerical value of רבה, and this is the meaning of מכה רבה." "<b>If.</b> If you are perceptive in the secret of the matter, you will find in it further, in the phrase מכה רבה ('a great blow'), an allusion to the great scourging which is the woman of Gehinnom, who draws power from the attribute of judgment called כה ('thus'); for this reason the plagues came upon Pharaoh with the word כה, and she too receives from the attribute of harsh, great, and strong judgment. Now the light and weak lashes of this world — behold, they are the sinner's atonement to rescue his soul from the hand of the flame, namely the great scourging called מכה רבה; and this is among the kindnesses of HaShem in commanding us to take this in place of that. And Scripture warns the one administering lashes not to add, for if he adds even one, he enters under its punishment of מכה רבה. This is: 'lest he add to strike him beyond these — מכה רבה.' These forty are redemption and atonement for the great scourging called רבה, and if he adds, he bears his punishment through it." "<b>And your brother shall be degraded before your eyes.</b> Until now it called him 'wicked' — 'and they shall declare the wicked guilty' (Deuteronomy 25:1), and 'if the wicked one be worthy of beating' (Deuteronomy 25:2) — but once he has received his sentence it calls him 'your brother.' So too did our Rabbis of blessed memory expound: 'ונקלה אחיך' — once he has been flogged, he is your brother. And from this verse our Sages of blessed memory proved in tractate Megillah that all those liable to כרת who received lashes are exempt from their כרת, as it is stated: 'ונקלה אחיך' — once he has been flogged, he is your brother. Now this exemption does not apply to our lashes, for our lashes are only so that the person be shamed, and shame is one of the forms of atonement. As the prophet upon him be peace said (Ezekiel 16:63): 'למען תזכרי ובשת, בכפרי לך לכל אשר עשית' — 'that you may remember and be ashamed, when I atone for you for all that you have done.' Rather, it is said concerning the lashes that were practiced in Talmudic times, when they would bind his hands to the pillar, as I mentioned above. And you will see that the word אחי\"ך has the numerical value of forty less one, and Scripture alludes to you that once he receives lashes equal to the count of אחיך, he is your brother. Likewise the word אלה is forty less one, together with the three letters."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {5 "The translation preserves the kabbalistic allusions (אשה של גיהנם, מדת הדין) literally without expansion, per the voice rules for Way of the Kabbalah passages.", 6 "The word אלה in gematria equals 36; 'forty less one' is 39, matching the three additional letters referenced. The translation preserves the gematria claim exactly as written without correction or verification."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:23.456001021Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}